#!/bin/sh
# knowledge-mcp — the MCP server entry the plugin registers (plugin.json, mcpServers.knowledge).
#
# A Claude session expects an MCP server to be a process it can talk to over stdin and stdout, and `exec` cannot fall back once it has run, so the launcher asks first which program to become. Three arrangements, best first:
#
#   1. The Rust binary: `tagents-knowledge join` connects to the one shared daemon (starting it when it is not running), relays stdin and stdout to it, and leaves the session with one process of a few megabytes and no node and no `nc`.
#   2. The shared node daemon: `tagents-knowledge serve` makes sure one daemon runs for this environment and prints the unix socket it listens on, and `nc -U` joins this session's stdin and stdout to it.
#   3. The node server of the session itself: `tagents-knowledge mcp`, a node process of about 30 MB per session.
#
# The probe on the next line runs `join` with no input and with its output thrown away, and the exit code says which arrangement this machine has. With the binary, the empty input connects, finds or starts the daemon, shuts the write half and exits 0, and the daemon is left running for the `join` that follows. The probe costs one more start of a small process; on a machine without the binary it costs a shell that exits, because the wrapper answers `join` with 69 before it starts any node.
#   0   the binary served the probe: become `join` for real.
#   69  the wrapper has no binary (none installed, another version, TAGENTS_NO_NATIVE=1): the old arrangement, silently.
#   2   a wrapper from before this arrangement, whose node program does not know `join`: the old arrangement, silently, at the price of one node start until the next `tagents update`.
#   any other code (1, 3, 126, 127, a signal): the binary was there and failed, or there is no tagents-knowledge at all. One line names the code on stderr, which Claude Code keeps in the log of an MCP server, and the old arrangement runs.
#
# `exec` so that the last program IS the process Claude started: closing its stdin ends the connection, and killing it needs no shell in between.
#
# The old arrangement is the way out: a checkout that predates `serve` prints "unknown command" and exits 2, a machine without a usable `nc` has nothing to exec, and a daemon that cannot start leaves no socket. In each the session still gets its own server.
tagents-knowledge join </dev/null >/dev/null 2>&1; rc=$?
case $rc in
  0) exec tagents-knowledge join ;;
  69 | 2) ;;
  *) printf 'knowledge-mcp: tagents-knowledge join exited %s, using the node server (tagents-knowledge join </dev/null shows why)\n' "$rc" >&2 ;;
esac
sock=$(tagents-knowledge serve 2>/dev/null) && [ -S "$sock" ] && command -v nc >/dev/null 2>&1 && exec nc -U "$sock"
exec tagents-knowledge mcp
